      "wp_id": 354,
      "title": "150th anniversary of the birth of Krastyo Sarafov",
      "year": "2026",
      "denomination": "5 euro",
      "metal": "Cu 999/1000",
      "diameter": "34.2",
      "front_image_url": "https://ivandewolf.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/225519-1.gif",
      "back_image_url": "https://ivandewolf.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/225519-2.gif",
      "description": "Krastyo Sarafov is a legend of the Bulgarian stage, who played over two hundred\r\ncharacters for more than half a century, one of the first professionally trained\r\nactors, whose stagecraft ranks him among the greatest European actors in the\r\nfirst half of the 20th century. A talented professional and a perfectionist, he went\r\ndeep into his roles, his acting proving the words shared with colleagues: ‘An actor\r\non the stage feels like a soldier on the battlefield, always ready for anything.’\r\nHe was born on 6 April 1876 in the village of Libyahovo, Nevrokop region (now\r\nIlinden village, Gotse Delchev region), in a large patriotic family with participants\r\nin the revolutionary movement and the fight for church independence. As early as\r\na student in Sofia, Krastyo became enamoured with theatre. His love led him to\r\nthe first performance in 1891. However, the family strongly opposed his desire\r\nand sent him abroad to continue studying in a place with no theatre. In 1893,\r\nSarafov graduated from the Bulgarian Men's High School of Adrianople, even\r\nthere performing on the school stage.\r\nIn 1895, he earned a scholarship to study drama abroad and graduated with\r\nhonours from the Imperial Theatre School in St Petersburg. He joined the first\r\nprofessional troupe ‘Tears and Laughter’, renamed to ‘Bulgarian National\r\nTheatre’ in 1904.\r\nAs a volunteer in the Balkan War, Krastyo Sarafov was wounded at the front.\r\nHe spent most of his long artistic career as an actor, director and manager in the\r\nNational Theatre and the theatres in Rousse, Burgas, Varna and Plovdiv, going\r\nalso on tours to the USA, Canada and major European stages. His unforgettable\r\nroles are dramatic, as well as comic in both foreign and Bulgarian plays along with\r\nradio theatre performances and several films.\r\nLoved by the audience and respected by all of society, Krastyo Sarafov was\r\nhonoured with numerous state awards. Since 1951, the Higher School of Theatre\r\n(now the National Academy of Theatre and Film Arts) in Sofia has borne his name,\r\nas has the National Theatre from 1951 to 1962. His last appearance on stage was\r\nin 1949, and on 27 August 1952, Bulgaria bid farewell to its favourite national\r\nactor.",
      "series_term_ids": [
        12
      ]
    },
