      "description": "The April Uprising of 1876 was the most important event in the national revolutionary movement, whereby the Bulgarian people declared clearly their right to be free and independent. This struggle gained momentum in the 70s of the 19th century through the establishment of revolutionary committees and national awakeners’ actions. The Stara Zagora Uprising (1875), although suppressed, was the harbinger of the rising revolutionary storm. In the aftermath, the Giurgiu Revolutionary Committee was created, and four revolutionary districts were formed. They were headed by the apostles Stefan Stambolov (Tarnovo), Ilarion Dragostinov (Sliven), Stoyan Zaimov (Vratsa) and Panayot Volov (Plovdiv – Panagyurishte). Despite extremely short preparations and insufficient weapons, on 14–16 April 1876, the Fourth (Panagyurishte) Revolutionary District repre- sentatives gathered in the Oborishte site near Panagyurishte where a decision was made to declare a national uprising on 1 May.  The uprising broke out prematurely because of betrayal: as early as on 20 April on the Kalachev Bridge (the First Gunshot Bridge) in Koprivshtitsa. On the same day, Todor Kableshkov sent the Bloody Letter to Panagyurishte, and Georgi Benkovski's Flying Squad (Hvurkovata Cheta) set out to raise the surrounding villages in the Panagyurishte District and mobilise the insurgents. The first (Tarnovo) revolutionary district also rose up en masse. The insurrection broke out in the other revolutionary districts, though on a much smaller scale, only with separate detachments, destroyed by the Ottoman army. This was also the fate of Hristo Botev’s detachment, which descended on the Kozloduy’s bank on 17 May. The Ottoman response was brutal: casualties numbered around 30,000, but the Uprising showed the world Bulgarian aspirations for freedom and independence and readiness to fight for them. A year later, the Russo-Turkish War was declared, known as the War of Bulgaria’s Liberation.",
